A||N||8||vtom||starting but stops||||8||
Z||000000||vtom||11-10-2000||07:45 PM||||we placed an '86 2.8L in an '84 and changed all wiring ect. now when it starts it only runs through one complete firing on all cylinders then dies like someone turned it off.  if we spray starter fluid as we start the car it will run as long as we continue to spray it.  we replaced the fuel pump and filter.  and have wired the fuel pump direct but still does the same thing. starts and runs till all 6 cylinders have fired. any suggestions anyone?||||reg||8
Z||000001||Raydar||11-10-2000||08:15 PM||||Check the fuses. There should be two injector fuses -one for each bank. Just a shot in the dark.||||reg||1
Z||000002||strdream||11-10-2000||09:16 PM||||Did you install a known good running engine? The first thing I would check is the mecanical stuff. Maybe as vacuum comes on to fuel pressure regulator it dumps. Maybe the fuel pump can make pressure but not enough volume. Test the fuel pressure anyway it is very critical. Clamp off the return line and try it again. Next go to the electrical. Make sure all of the sensor wires are making goog contact. Fuel puls command is from an RPM referance. Crank sensor, Cam, some use ignition for referance. I don't know what your car has, I am just speeking in general terms if that helps. Puls width is established based on input from Map, Maf, Engine Temp, Air charge temp, and oxigine sensor. I am telling you this to help you think it thru. For example if the engine temp wire is shorted out it will tell the computor that the engine is hot, computor than delivers a very lean fuel mixture. Not enough to run on.  If you can get your hands on a noid light and test if there is an injector puls present you can rule out lots of possibilities.<P>Ross   ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||11-10-2000||11:09 PM||||Not to insult you, but you did put a v-6 fuel pump in, right?||||reg||1
Z||000004||vtom||11-11-2000||10:58 AM||||we changed over all the electrical wiring and the fuse block from the donor car and changed all fuses. thanks for the shot.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||vtom||11-11-2000||11:01 AM||||no insult to me.  had to run 70 miles to get the v6 fuel pump and filters.  changed out the donor cars gas tank and fuel lines (they were in better shape than the one for the 4 popper. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||vtom||11-11-2000||11:10 AM||||We got the donor car about a year ago and the engine was a good runner.  Used the donor for body panels and interior parts for another project.  the clutch and trany were shot but engine sounded great.  we didn't fire it before the changeover but did know that it ran good before.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||11-11-2000||11:28 AM||||The key is that it runs off the starting fluid, which isn't exactly good for it so try not to keep doing that.<P>That means there's no fuel inject. 3 things can cause that.<P>Check Fuel presure on the rail. You could have the fuel lines hooked up backward or the pressure regulator failed open.<P>no inject power. you checked the fuses. pull the harness off a couple injectors and see if there's power when the key is on. The pink and pink/white wires should have power. (The injectors fire when the ECM closes a switch to ground on the other wire.)<P>Next connect an analog meter or a test light across the 2 injector terminals. (They make test lights, called noid lights, especially for this. You can get them at many parts stores.) The light should flash when the injector is suposed to fire.<P>If the light is flashing then the ECM should be OK. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||vtom||11-11-2000||08:51 PM||||Thanks for the input on putting my '84 back in running condition.  The information I received helped to make me check further into wiring. Fusable links can be a problem. Its running great!!!! ||||reg||10
